  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0215\" contains 7 tables. The main purpose of this schema is to manage the information about passengers, airports, aircrafts, flight crews, flights, tickets, and luggage.\nThe \"PASSENGER\" table represents the passengers who use the service. It has an AccountID as the primary key, which is an integer and cannot be null. It also includes columns for the PassengerName, PassengerAge, and FrequentflyerID, all of which are of varying length.\nThe \"AIRPORT\" table holds information about airports. The AirportCode is the primary key, which is a varchar of length 8. It also includes columns for AirportName, AirportCity, AirportState, AirportAddress, Classification, and TimeZone.\nThe \"AIRCRAFT\" table contains information about aircrafts. The AirplaneNumber is the primary key, which is a varchar of length 30. It also includes columns for AirplaneMake, WeightLimit, FlightNumber, and AirportCode. The AirportCode is a foreign key referencing the AIRPORT table.\nThe \"FLIGHTCREW\" table contains information about flight crew members. The EmployeeID is the primary key, which is an integer and cannot be null. It also includes columns for EmployeeName, EmployeeTitle, EmployeeAge, EmployeeStreetAddress, EmployeeZipCode, EmployeeCity, EmployeeState, and AirportCode. The AirportCode is a foreign key referencing the AIRPORT table.\nThe \"FLIGHT\" table contains information about flights. The FlightNumber is the primary key, which is a varchar of length 30. It also includes columns for DepartureTime, ArrivalTime, FlightPrice, AirportCode, GateNumber, AirplaneNumber, and EmployeeID. The AirplaneNumber is a foreign key referencing the AIRCRAFT table and EmployeeID is a foreign key referencing the FLIGHTCREW table.\nThe \"TICKET\" table contains information about tickets. The TicketID is the primary key, which is a varchar of length 20. It also includes columns for FromLocation, ToLocation, Seat, Price, FlightStatus, Class, AccountID, and FlightNumber. The AccountID is a foreign key referencing the PASSENGER table and FlightNumber is a foreign key referencing the FLIGHT table.\nThe \"LUGGAGE\" table contains information about luggage. The LuggageID is the primary key, which is a varchar of length 20. It also includes columns for CarouselNumber, Weight, AccountID, and AirplaneNumber. The AccountID is a foreign key referencing the PASSENGER table and AirplaneNumber is a foreign key referencing the AIRCRAFT table.\nThis schema ensures referential integrity by having foreign keys that reference the primary keys of other tables. It also ensures entity integrity by having primary keys that uniquely identify each record in the table. The schema is designed to ensure data integrity by enforcing constraints on the data types and lengths of the columns.",
  "gold_question": "American Airlines Operations Application\n\n1) Background / Context\nAmerican Airlines requires a comprehensive application to support their day-to-day operations. The system is intended to manage critical data related to passengers, airport activities, aircraft, flight crew, and flight logistics using a MySQL database.\n\n2) Objectives\n- To streamline management of flight operations, crew, and ticketing.\n- To enhance data accessibility and management for operational efficiency.\n- To integrate diverse operational components within a unified system.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Airline Operations Managers\n- Flight Crew Members\n- Ticketing Agents\n- Passengers\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n- Manage passenger information, including account details and frequent flyer data.\n- Maintain airport details, including codes, names, and locations.\n- Track aircraft details such as make, weight limits, and assigned flight numbers.\n- Handle flight crew details including personal information and assigned airport.\n- Schedule and manage flight information including departure, arrival times, and pricing.\n- Issue tickets with route specifics, seating, pricing, and flight status.\n- Transport luggage management, correlating with passenger and flight details.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Ensure data integrity and referential integrity across all entities.\n- Support efficient data retrieval to handle operational demands.\n- Maintain system security for sensitive data related to flights and passengers.\n- Achieve high availability and reliability to support continuous airline operations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0215.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0215\nCREATE TABLE `passenger` (\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `passenger_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `passenger_age` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `frequentflyer_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`account_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `airport` (\n  `airport_code` varchar(8) NOT NULL,\n  `airport_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_city` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_state` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `classification` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `time_zone` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`airport_code`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `aircraft` (\n  `airplane_number` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `airplane_make` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `weight_limit` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `flight_number` varchar(30) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(8) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`airplane_number`),\n  CONSTRAINT `aircraft_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airport_code`) REFERENCES `airport` (`airport_code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `flightcrew` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_title` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_age` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_street_address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_zip_code` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_city` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_state` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(8) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `flightcrew_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airport_code`) REFERENCES `airport` (`airport_code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `flight` (\n  `flight_number` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `departure_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `arrival_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `flight_price` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airport_code` varchar(8) NOT NULL,\n  `gate_number` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `airplane_number` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`flight_number`),\n  CONSTRAINT `flight_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airplane_number`) REFERENCES `aircraft` (`airplane_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `flight_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `flightcrew` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticket` (\n  `ticket_id` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `from_location` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `to_location` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `seat` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `flight_status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `class` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `flight_number` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ticket_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticket_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `passenger` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticket_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`flight_number`) REFERENCES `flight` (`flight_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `luggage` (\n  `luggage_id` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `carousel_number` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `weight` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `airplane_number` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`luggage_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `luggage_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `passenger` (`account_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `luggage_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`airplane_number`) REFERENCES `aircraft` (`airplane_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
